I mean, it's a huge place. I've only been here a year, and I've learned so much. And I think

01:30.180 --> 01:34.740
really maybe no one knows everything that happens at Reuters. But there's three kind of big pieces

01:34.740 --> 01:39.540
to the company that the outsider might not know. One is that service that John talked about

01:39.540 --> 01:44.500
that's so famous that sends photos and breaking news to more than 2,000 newsrooms around the world

01:44.500 --> 01:49.740
in all different languages. That's thing one. Thing two, which shouldn't be overlooked,

01:49.740 --> 01:55.540
is that Reuters is the exclusive news provider to one of the world's major financial terminal

01:55.540 --> 02:00.560
businesses, which has recently been rebranded as LSEG. It used to be known as Refinitiv.

02:01.000 --> 02:05.060
And that is, in many ways, kind of the Pepsi to the Coca-Cola of the Bloomberg terminal,

02:05.060 --> 02:10.220
if you're familiar with that. And then, of course, there's Reuters.com, which is where anyone can go

02:10.220 --> 02:15.660
in for free, read a selection of our breaking news, and our excellent investigative special reports.

02:16.060 --> 02:20.480
So those are kind of the three big pieces and arms of the company to keep in mind as we move through

02:20.480 --> 02:26.740
this. And at the core of all of it is really the lifeblood of the Reuters business, as it has been

02:26.740 --> 02:31.220
since the very beginning, which is breaking news, right? You know, literally, there are hundreds,

02:31.460 --> 02:36.680
if not thousands, of little itty-bitty stories that are flying out from our offices around the world

02:36.680 --> 02:41.660
every day. And it's been that way for more than a century. One of my favorite illustrations at this

02:41.660 --> 02:47.940
point is this very, very real 1883 memo sent out from the London office where John now works

02:47.940 --> 02:53.640
to the correspondents in the field, urging them to deliver more breaking news. I won't read the

02:53.640 --> 02:58.440
whole thing to you, though it is an entertaining read in general. One thing to keep in mind is back

02:58.440 --> 03:04.100
then the telegram was how breaking news was submitted. And the author of this memo is really pushing down

03:04.100 --> 03:09.880
on the troops to deliver more breaking news. Near the end, they actually give an enumerated list of

03:09.880 --> 03:15.280
the type of news that they would like to hear more of. My favorite little bit here, if you slow down

03:15.280 --> 03:23.580
and squint and read, is a call for all disturbances arising from strikes, duels between and suicides of

03:23.580 --> 03:29.740
persons of great note, social or political, and murders of a sensational or atrocious character,

03:29.740 --> 03:34.480
which maybe gives you a sense of how editors saw the news back then. It's still true to some degree

03:34.480 --> 03:41.160
today. But the key line in this memo that is quoted around the office quite frequently comes

03:41.160 --> 03:47.500
in that last paragraph where it is requested that the bare fact be first telegraphed with the utmost

03:47.500 --> 03:53.460
promptitude. And we say the line with a little bit of irony today, given the sort of elevated,

03:53.460 --> 03:59.860
you know, British diction, the king's English in there. But it really is still true and really the

03:59.860 --> 04:07.040
essence of our business. Being fast, being first, being simple and clear is really the primary

04:07.040 --> 04:11.620
imperative of the company. It's the company, we do so much more than that, but it really is at the

04:11.620 --> 04:17.560
heart and soul. And it's that kind of mission that we're going to talk about today and how Data

04:17.560 --> 04:24.180
Wrapper helps us serve it better. And we're going to do that one by John giving an overview of how,

04:24.180 --> 04:29.500
you know, he and his team built this, I think, truly wonderful system for distributing Data Wrapper

04:29.500 --> 04:35.260
charts across all those different arms we talked about. And then how I came in and tried to do a

04:35.260 --> 04:39.640
little, tried to do even more with it. So, John, you want to do part one? You want to take it, man?

04:40.080 --> 04:42.280
I'll take it. If you don't mind driving.

04:42.280 --> 04:51.500
So, like a lot of places, Data Wrapper filled a pretty predictable need for us in our newsroom.

04:51.660 --> 04:58.840
We are, I suppose, one of the larger graphics teams by comparison to folks who aren't working

04:58.840 --> 05:04.340
in some of the mainstay newsrooms, but still compared to the number of journalists that we

05:04.340 --> 05:09.200
work with, we're actually quite small. And so, for Data Wrapper, we had all the same problems,

05:09.200 --> 05:15.160
so I'm sure you've heard Echo Gravel's conference about, you know, being very thinly spread. It always

05:15.160 --> 05:20.620
seemed like we couldn't quite keep up with the reporting that the newsroom was doing and really

05:20.620 --> 05:27.060
sort of failing that first rung of our own company ethos to really be there for the big breaking news.

05:27.780 --> 05:34.140
And behind the scenes, the reporters and editors that we work with would often reach out if they

05:34.140 --> 05:40.760
couldn't get us to these really terrible kind of charting tools that would be sort of scattered

05:40.760 --> 05:47.680
throughout dark corners of Reuters infrastructure. I then found a very nice copy down there.

05:47.980 --> 05:53.540
I believe that is a real chart that's come out of one of the terrible systems. I think Icon is that one.

05:53.540 --> 06:02.200
And so, in a lot of ways, Data Wrapper was going to fill exactly the need that we all have in situations

06:02.200 --> 06:08.260
that we really need to scale out the number of people who can create charts in the newsroom for the next one.

06:10.160 --> 06:15.060
If you would, Ben, I think you're driving or am I, or can I drive, actually? Okay, cool.

06:15.060 --> 06:21.800
So, as Ben mentioned, though, we're a little bit different because of our wire service business.

06:21.980 --> 06:27.320
And one of the main tenets of that is that we have to service other media clients.

06:28.560 --> 06:33.980
And so, in addition to parking charts on a website, we actually also have to distribute them in a number

06:33.980 --> 06:40.680
of ways to other media who will then publish them on in their websites, papers, television segments,

06:40.680 --> 06:47.580
whatever, go ahead and pull, I guess I'll say. Or can I?

06:54.220 --> 06:56.180
I think you're going to have to drive, Ben. Sorry about that.

06:56.780 --> 07:07.380
So, at Reuters, we publish charts in a lot of different flavors for our media clients in particular.

07:07.380 --> 07:13.680
And so, we call these different additions, some of which you'll be very familiar with coming straight out of Data Wrapper.

07:13.860 --> 07:18.440
So, embeddable HTML pages. So, the chart that you can just embed with embed code.

07:18.800 --> 07:22.580
We also make those so that our media clients can host them on their own servers.

07:23.040 --> 07:27.720
But we also make static and editable images. So, PNGs, PDFs.

07:27.980 --> 07:32.400
We also make our source code for all of our graphics available to clients.

07:32.400 --> 07:37.760
We have clients who have developers in-house who like to customize things themselves.

07:38.120 --> 07:43.900
And then we do the usual kind of light, dark mode charts, little variations like that pull.

07:44.540 --> 07:52.920
But what that all means for us is that that little publish now button is not the end for us.

07:52.920 --> 08:02.300
Basically, that's just the start of a process by which we have to take our charts and make them ready for media clients.

08:02.520 --> 08:07.760
And to do that, we use Data Wrapper's publishing hooks in the API.

08:08.520 --> 08:15.340
Basically, what that does is it gives us the ability to, you know, suck out those charts and the various flavors and formats that we need.

08:15.340 --> 08:24.420
We can break open the HTML, CSS, and JS to do various things in them, repackage them the way that we need them to be packaged for our clients and ship them out.

08:24.840 --> 08:33.400
And that may sound like it's very specific to us, but I think it opens up a ton of options for anybody to do really, really cool things with Data Wrapper charts.

08:34.120 --> 08:37.880
We use AWS Lambdas so that this can really scale.

08:37.980 --> 08:41.120
We'll talk about how big it scales here in a second with Ben.

08:41.120 --> 08:48.600
But that post-processing step is really key to us being able to service our clients.

08:49.460 --> 08:50.820
You can go ahead and get your answer.

08:51.200 --> 08:54.160
We also use custom fields in the chart editor.

08:54.340 --> 08:59.220
It's a really easy way for us to attach some of the vital metadata.

08:59.960 --> 09:01.960
You'll see these roots and wild slugs.

09:02.100 --> 09:04.200
These are some of the weirdest nomenclature hats.

09:04.560 --> 09:10.980
I think these are basically little pieces of metadata that allow us to categorize and make it easier for the clients.

09:11.120 --> 09:15.940
And then once we've done all that, we've tagged our things, we've repackaged them, we've pushed them all up.

09:16.020 --> 09:24.940
We can ship them out to the world through a platform that we call Reuters Connect, which is where clients can come and purchase our charts in any one of the formats that I mentioned.

09:24.940 --> 09:37.160
As part of all that process, while we're doing all this repackaging, we take the moment to go ahead and flag charts that are coming, that our newsroom has made or that our team has made or that our bots have made.

09:37.160 --> 09:42.060
And especially in the context of our newsroom, it's actually a really great moment.

09:42.320 --> 09:45.560
And I think I've heard several people mention this and deal with Slack or whatever.

09:46.200 --> 09:53.780
It's a really great moment for us to coach our newsroom and really have sort of a feedback cycle about the charts as people are making.

09:53.780 --> 09:57.380
And this is one such example from some of the newsroom.

09:58.220 --> 10:08.300
And if you are, so at the end of the day, what that means is in the last 18 months, we've onboarded something north of 800 reporters, editors, and producers across our newsroom.

10:08.900 --> 10:10.900
They're publishing dozens of charts every day.

10:10.900 --> 10:19.580
And what that means is that the load is off of our, by comparison, small graphics, so we can focus on certain bigger things that we're known for, where we start to come back graphics.

10:20.140 --> 10:25.100
That also means that reporters can do more niche work in their individual beats.

10:25.580 --> 10:29.760
And our producers have actually become some of the most important chart makers we have.

10:29.860 --> 10:34.640
The first one is getting out maps, quick charts on the news as it's happening.

10:34.640 --> 10:47.140
So once we have that sort of pipeline in place, one of the things that we started to try to do was do some of the sort of maybe chart building that you've heard some of the others here talking about.

10:47.220 --> 10:52.400
This is our first example is done by Trebs Hartman in our graphics team.

10:52.500 --> 10:54.380
It's called Tremolo, which I think is a good name for it.

10:54.380 --> 10:56.780
It's a USGS powered earthquake monitor.

10:56.780 --> 11:13.800
So the, one of my favorite examples of this was last year, the Morocco quiz struck, we had the shape maps out within minutes, and we continued to pivot on those throughout the day, had more invitations to them, stuff like that.

11:15.020 --> 11:26.640
So we've now had this really great system by which we can, you know, either by hand from the newsroom or through an automated process, create a chart, and then publish it out into the world in all these various ways.

11:27.220 --> 11:30.000
And so we are ready to try scaling this thing way, way up.

11:30.100 --> 11:31.160
And that's where Ben comes in.

11:31.400 --> 11:31.540
Ben?

11:32.580 --> 11:33.340
Yeah, that's right.

11:33.420 --> 11:39.980
So, I mean, all this work was, you know, kind of ready and waiting for me when I started at Reuters, and I was trying to figure out what the heck am I going to do?

11:40.040 --> 11:43.580
Let's find something ambitious, a project to take on, something to chew on.

11:43.980 --> 11:47.740
And I found inspiration immediately from two places.

11:48.400 --> 11:53.580
One is a system that was invented here at the company a few years ago called Insight.

11:53.580 --> 11:58.360
And so this system is basically templated text stories.

11:58.840 --> 12:08.160
So when new data comes in on the feeds, they're sort of fit like a Madlib into a templated story and then sent directly out into the wire.

12:08.160 --> 12:18.420
And this is a type of system that exists not just at Reuters but at other news wires and has been very effective at increasing kind of the speed and scale of the text operation.

12:18.420 --> 12:29.040
The graphics editor, Matt Weber, you know, approached me and he said that he and others had had the longstanding idea of why can't we do the same thing for graphics, right?

12:29.120 --> 12:36.760
So if we can automate these Madlib text stories, why can't we automate the graphics that would go with the same thing as well?

12:36.760 --> 12:40.020
And I thought, man, he's really got a point.

12:40.380 --> 12:42.040
So let's look at it.

12:42.080 --> 12:43.180
Let's see if we can make it happen.

12:43.900 --> 12:49.540
And then at that point, I learned all about John's system, you know, which he just, I think, covered very well.

12:49.760 --> 12:51.080
And it kind of blew my mind.

12:51.080 --> 13:01.820
It's really just a powerful, you know, end-to-end kind of assembly line that's just waiting for widgets to kind of be pushed down it.

13:02.220 --> 13:10.720
Someone who can claim to have worked for the entire tenure of the Tronk company, it reminded me in some ways in my lighter moments of the funnel,

13:11.280 --> 13:16.360
which is one of the more hilarious, unintentionally hilarious metaphors in journalism history.

13:16.920 --> 13:19.120
If you haven't seen the video, the link's right there.

13:19.340 --> 13:19.900
Give it a look.

13:19.900 --> 13:25.340
And I just needed to figure out how I could get the data in there.

13:25.560 --> 13:28.520
So, you know, on the one end is the Data Wrapper API.

13:29.320 --> 13:37.760
If we could put charts into the Data Wrapper API, the top of the funnel, so to speak, we know they'll flow entirely down through John's system.

13:38.300 --> 13:42.840
And as has been covered by other speakers in this panel, that's totally doable, right?

13:44.080 --> 13:46.720
And then I'm a Python guy, not an R guy.

13:46.720 --> 13:57.080
I found, wow, there's this cool Python wrapper for the Data Wrapper API that makes it easy with a couple lines of Python code to pass in whatever chart you might want.

13:57.380 --> 13:58.300
Oh, okay, that's great.

13:58.780 --> 14:00.720
Started checking that out, started working with that.

14:00.980 --> 14:03.660
Sergio Sanchez-Zavala is the inventor of that, by the way.

14:03.920 --> 14:04.360
Cool guy.

14:04.360 --> 14:10.360
And then I saw on the other end, well, where are we going to get the data to put into the charts, right?

14:10.360 --> 14:12.680
Well, we've got this terminal, okay?

14:12.780 --> 14:23.200
So this LSEG terminal system really is this kind of incredible database that has limited access to a very small number of financial professionals who pay for it.

14:23.200 --> 14:32.580
But it updates literally within seconds of new market data being posted or macroeconomic indicators appearing on government websites.

14:33.360 --> 14:39.300
And learning about it has really been eye-opening and interesting to me as someone who's never worked at a terminal business before.

14:39.300 --> 14:51.620
The truth of it is, is behind a system like this is literally hundreds, if not thousands, of web scrapers that are crawling government data sites and putting in new figures instantly when they arrive.

14:51.820 --> 14:55.820
And then a whole quality control layer of people who make sure they flow through.

14:56.080 --> 14:59.420
So we had this great delivery system for graphics on the other.

14:59.540 --> 15:03.060
And we had this great data source, you know, on the other end.

15:03.120 --> 15:04.240
We just needed to connect them.

15:04.240 --> 15:14.200
Well, it turns out there's a Python API for the terminal as well, which would allow anyone with access to write code to download the data from the terminal.

15:14.720 --> 15:22.260
And all we needed to do was to kind of close the missing link between the data source, the terminal, and data wrapper, our delivery device.

15:22.380 --> 15:27.940
And that really was the work that needed to be done and was kind of the job for me if we were going to make it happen.

15:27.940 --> 15:39.540
And, you know, one way of thinking about a process like that, that kind of ferries data from one to the other, is what, you know, really gnarly, boring computer programmers call an ETL pipeline, right?

15:39.880 --> 15:41.920
I think it's good we're just calling them pipelines now.

15:42.000 --> 15:42.840
It's a little more human.

15:43.260 --> 15:47.260
But that's an acronym for extract, transform, and load, right?

15:47.360 --> 15:55.220
And what those typically do is the extractor, you know, downloads the source data from wherever it comes from, in this case, our terminal, right?

15:55.220 --> 16:06.740
There's some sort of computer programming layer that is going to transform, clean, summarize, reformat that data so that it's ready for delivery to kind of its target.

16:07.340 --> 16:12.420
And then the loading step is that process of then delivering it out, in this case, to, say, data wrapper.

16:13.120 --> 16:16.500
And so I set out to write something like that.

16:16.580 --> 16:22.280
In real life, the actual pipeline is a little more complicated, but that's really the essence of it.

16:22.280 --> 16:29.860
We're extracting from the terminal, we're transforming it into the format for data wrapper, and then we're posting to the API.

16:30.460 --> 16:47.120
The main sort of additional thing is sort of configuring each of your chart templates to behave in a certain way based on what the data source is, what type of chart you want, how you want to manage the layout, which we call the generation or the configuration of the template.

16:47.120 --> 16:51.180
Here is an example of one from real life.

16:51.300 --> 16:53.100
This is a screenshot from our code base.

16:53.440 --> 17:05.820
At the end of the day, we basically have kind of a Python framework of classes designed for each of those stages in the ETL pipeline that we then configure based on the template or chart.

17:05.820 --> 17:15.460
So this example, without belaboring it, is the unemployment chart that ran last Friday morning when the Bureau of Labor Statistics posted the new report.

17:15.920 --> 17:21.180
And again, if you were to squint and look carefully, you would see that it's really only about 20 lines of code.

17:21.180 --> 17:40.240
And most of it is really just the specific stuff related to what data file, what data series should I go get from the terminal, what should the headline be, you know, and what the date range should be and some of the specifics of how the chart ought to be, you know, collected and rendered.

17:40.240 --> 17:44.000
And that's really all it takes to do to do to do to do an individual chart.

17:44.500 --> 17:51.580
Those the system that executes that sort of pipeline process is run entirely in GitHub Actions.

17:51.820 --> 17:54.560
If you haven't checked out GitHub Actions yet, you should.

17:54.560 --> 18:14.540
This is a free task running system within the GitHub sort of repository universe where your code repository has access for free to task running jobs in data centers that will do on a schedule or based on triggers you set, run computer programming tasks.

18:14.540 --> 18:24.160
And so for each of our kind of we have our we have our templates sort of organized into groups around, say, revenue or jobs or inflation.

18:24.280 --> 18:28.980
And those groups of templates are then executed on a schedule very frequently.

18:29.340 --> 18:36.280
And when they detect that new data has been found in our source, the terminal, they make the chart and send it out to Data Wrapper.

18:37.560 --> 18:40.100
And it's that is it is the production environment.

18:40.100 --> 18:41.320
It works flawlessly.

18:41.320 --> 18:44.880
I recommend exploring things like this in your own work.

18:45.640 --> 18:50.640
Once they then deliver that to the Data Wrapper API, John's system takes over.

18:50.880 --> 18:58.180
And so this is the chart that got pushed out just one minute after the new numbers were posted to the BLS website last Friday morning.

18:58.380 --> 19:04.420
It flowed into this is the graphics pool where an editor could find, select the graphic and then embed it into a piece.

19:04.680 --> 19:05.920
It was there and ready.

19:05.920 --> 19:12.660
We're seeing these charts get delivered through the system faster than even the text, the text stories get written and edited.

19:12.900 --> 19:19.240
And they're often they're ahead of when the reporters and editors are even thinking about looking for art.

19:19.240 --> 19:22.920
So these are obviously speed wins.

19:22.920 --> 19:34.760
That chart is then put right into the story, which was on the homepage of Reuters.com and out on the wire in the next few minutes, as were several other charts in the jobs report, because we have a whole kind of package of four or five.

19:35.380 --> 19:36.580
But it's not just for speed.

19:36.580 --> 19:38.620
Indeed, you also begin to benefit from scale.

19:39.200 --> 19:46.280
So that same that same class that I showed earlier, you may have noticed that had two entries, one for the United States and the other for Canada.

19:46.280 --> 19:51.680
So that same template can be adapted to a different data series with a slightly different configuration.

19:52.420 --> 19:55.980
And boom, you now are doing two instead of one with the same amount of labor.

19:56.540 --> 20:01.340
And that can multiply to really, really large numbers in the right circumstance.

20:01.920 --> 20:05.580
So, for instance, we have a template for a bar chart of corporate earnings.

20:05.580 --> 20:12.620
So every quarter, a public corporation will report to its shareholders how much money it made in the previous period.

20:13.000 --> 20:14.460
We can make the same bar chart.

20:14.540 --> 20:27.180
We are making the same bar chart for every single publicly listed company we choose to track, which allows us to make literally thousands of these charts within seconds or minutes after the data being discovered in the system.

20:27.560 --> 20:31.840
And that's where you begin to get to scale that no human is going to be able to do on their own.

20:31.900 --> 20:32.920
So it's not just faster.

20:33.100 --> 20:35.500
It can do quite a bit more.

20:35.580 --> 20:46.960
And the result of that is that the staff on John's team is freed up to do much higher value, higher skill work, which can also have much, much higher impact as well.

20:46.960 --> 21:02.020
So in real life, you know, that once a month, every Friday morning, Allie Levine here in the New York office would have to wake up early and make that same batch of jobs reports every single time unless she missed her alarm clock, I suppose.

21:02.020 --> 21:03.020
But now the robot does.

21:03.020 --> 21:11.160
But now the robot does that for her and she gets to sleep in and do things like this beautiful Walt Disney graphic they recently published instead.

21:11.160 --> 21:23.560
And of, you know, all the people and writers that I've talked to about this project, no one has been more excited about it than Allie, who sees it as really something that allows her to work on more rewarding and challenging work.

21:23.560 --> 21:34.940
We have kind of an internal system that we've built for monitoring the output thus far, we have close to 60 templates that we've deployed,

21:34.940 --> 21:39.020
including even a few that branch out to different inputs and outputs.

21:39.020 --> 21:43.940
So for instance, we have some custom web scrapers that power charts that don't use the terminal.

21:43.940 --> 21:52.820
And we actually are publishing some static images generated not via data wrapper that get embedded into a morning email newsletter series.

21:52.820 --> 21:57.320
So the pipeline system can also kind of horizontally scale, if you will, in that way.

21:58.320 --> 22:04.340
Thus far, we've we've pushed through about 20,000 charts to data wrapper in the system.

22:04.840 --> 22:09.540
That's according to my little profile row in our our team, our team page and data wrapper.

22:11.000 --> 22:18.680
My hope is if we aren't already, we will we will have published more charts than anyone else on the platform using using this system.

22:18.680 --> 22:27.680
Beyond the actual work here privately inside of Reuters, I partnered with Sergio, the the maintainer of the Python data wrapper library.

22:27.680 --> 22:39.680
And we recently have released a new version, which I think maybe we're announcing for the first time here today now covers 100 percent of the API endpoints on the data wrapper API.

22:39.680 --> 22:49.680
So anything that's documented in the data wrapper API, we from creating folders and moving folders, organizing teams, creating maps, all of it should be possible in the system.

22:49.680 --> 22:52.680
If something isn't, tell me and I'll make sure we get it in.

22:52.680 --> 22:59.680
And Sergio has been really great to work with on that for anyone who's really into API stuff.

22:59.680 --> 23:04.680
Here's this like a quick example of of how the Python wrapper could make a really basic chart.

23:04.680 --> 23:19.680
For those familiar with the API, you'll see that that metadata variable is queuing very closely to the sort of the JSON structure that the that the data wrapper prefers to be posted in.

23:19.680 --> 23:28.680
The result of that is that it's a little it's just a little verbose and and maybe not as friendly to beginners as it could be.

23:28.680 --> 23:43.680
So, you know, one of my personal kind of dreams or goals and I would love to get feedback and collaborators on this on anyone is interesting interested would be to maybe pursue an upgrade to the data wrapper API that is more say declarative.

23:43.680 --> 23:46.680
This this is pseudo code that does not exist.

23:46.680 --> 24:02.680
But to me, this is maybe how I think the API to accept Python code where all of the attributes are sort of keyword inputs that they all would be fully documented and listed out somewhere, which I'm not sure that they are currently.

24:02.680 --> 24:08.680
And just generally trying to move towards something that makes automation easier and easier over time.

24:08.680 --> 24:13.680
So if anyone has thoughts or or interest in that, I would I would love to hear what people think.

24:13.680 --> 24:16.680
This is kind of my little, you know, attempted dreaming it.

24:16.680 --> 24:31.680
And then finally, Sergio and I recently prepared a tutorial on how any beginner could pick up Python and begin automating data wrapper charts with with zero experience.

24:31.680 --> 24:38.680
We presented that one week ago today at the the night car conference, the large data journalism conference in Baltimore.

24:38.680 --> 24:47.680
It was standing room only there was there was so much enthusiasm from the data journalists we met to do more with data wrapper and to do more with automation.

24:47.680 --> 24:52.680
I'll share the link to this in that once I finished talking here, it is free to anyone.

24:52.680 --> 24:58.680
And every step in the class is explained and all of the code is included as well.

24:58.680 --> 25:01.680
I think that's what we have.

25:01.680 --> 25:03.680
