25:18.680 --> 25:19.680
All right.

25:19.680 --> 25:21.680
Thank you so much, Ben and John.

25:21.680 --> 25:23.680
It was a fantastic speech.

25:23.680 --> 25:24.680
Very informative.

25:24.680 --> 25:32.680
Great to see behind the curtain and learn so much about the intricacies of what goes into pushing out 20,000 charts.

25:32.680 --> 25:35.680
20,000 charts in about six months.

25:35.680 --> 25:41.680
That turnaround time that you mentioned is really remarkable and very impressive.

25:41.680 --> 25:47.680
I have a couple of questions while we wait for other questions to come through in the chat.

25:47.680 --> 25:50.680
Things that came to me while I was listening to this presentation.

25:50.680 --> 25:59.680
You mentioned a team's integration as a kind of plaxton to make sure that things were being reviewed or make sure that nothing was going wrong.

25:59.680 --> 26:03.680
So was that an idea for this project from the get go?

26:03.680 --> 26:11.680
Or was that implemented after the core pipeline was sort of built and created?

26:11.680 --> 26:13.680
Yeah, no, that was part of the original project.

26:13.680 --> 26:20.680
I mean, we needed to feed back some information to reporters about where their charts went into the graphic system.

26:20.680 --> 26:26.680
And just decided that was a really great point to go ahead and pop an image of the chart since we had that in hand already.

26:26.680 --> 26:31.680
And that gave us a chance to see what people were making very easily throughout our day.

26:31.680 --> 26:33.680
And we could pop in some comments and stuff like that.

26:33.680 --> 26:45.680
So yeah, it's actually been one of the funnest things about the whole process for me because you never know what kind of data you're going to see on any given day come through.

26:45.680 --> 26:51.680
And it's nice to talk back to the newsroom about what charts are breaking.

26:51.680 --> 26:55.680
Really true.

26:55.680 --> 27:00.680
It's also one of my favorite parts about this job is the not knowing what kind of charts are going to come through on any given day.

27:00.680 --> 27:04.680
It's very exciting and agree with you there and great foresight on that.

27:04.680 --> 27:08.680
We do have a question from Nora Gully.

27:08.680 --> 27:11.680
How do you byline automated charts?

27:11.680 --> 27:15.680
We don't put a byline in the data wrapper field.

27:15.680 --> 27:16.680
We list the source.

27:16.680 --> 27:20.680
If, you know, if the data comes from the terminal, we list LSEG as the source, of course.

27:20.680 --> 27:29.680
But, you know, no chart is published without a human editor reviewing it and what in the language of Reuters packaging it with the story.

27:29.680 --> 27:35.680
And so and so, you know, we kind of view that as the editorial oversight on the piece.

27:35.680 --> 27:45.680
If you're interested in how the quality control aspect further, I mean, we do have a committee of people that I review all the templates with before they're implemented.

27:45.680 --> 27:50.680
And so these are people who are experts in the domain that we're trying to make the chart for.

27:50.680 --> 27:56.680
And so they're involved in kind of a review process prior to that.

27:56.680 --> 27:57.680
Okay.

27:57.680 --> 27:59.680
That makes sense.

27:59.680 --> 28:00.680
Okay.

28:00.680 --> 28:03.680
I do have one other question that came to me.

28:03.680 --> 28:07.680
You mentioned that a lot of your data comes from one data source.

28:07.680 --> 28:15.680
Is that always in the same consistent format or is that ever cleaning that and preparing it for data wrapper?

28:15.680 --> 28:17.680
I can imagine that would be a sticking point.

28:17.680 --> 28:20.680
Is that always the same cleaning process?

28:20.680 --> 28:22.680
It's mostly consistent.

28:22.680 --> 28:27.680
You know, it's a truly massive database with a really wide array of data.

28:27.680 --> 28:30.680
And so it is definitely not a hundred percent consistent.

28:30.680 --> 28:35.680
And there can be, and across domains, there can be a little bit of quirks.

28:35.680 --> 28:49.680
Like for instance, commodities data is sort of, that is handled a little different from stock market data, which is handled a little different from the macroeconomic data in terms of what the columns are called or how you query it from the source API.

28:49.680 --> 28:53.680
And so that's where the Python pipeline kind of comes in.

28:53.680 --> 29:00.680
And the approach we've taken is to try to write kind of generic Python classes that handle the most common kind of variations.

29:00.680 --> 29:06.680
Hey, here's our monthly macroeconomic extractor and transformer, right?

29:06.680 --> 29:19.680
And that's going to handle most of the macroeconomic data, but then you can always kind of override or tweak, you know, the individual implementations based on what the quirks might be.

29:19.680 --> 29:21.680
Yeah, makes sense.

29:21.680 --> 29:26.680
Having that kind of room to adjust things with human eyes if needed.

29:26.680 --> 29:27.680
Fantastic.

